## What REST Actually Is

REST isn't a protocol or standard—it's an architectural **style** defined by Roy Fielding in his 2000 PhD dissertation. The core idea: treat everything as a **resource** (user, order, product) that can be manipulated using standard HTTP operations.

Think of it like a library catalog system. Each book (resource) has a unique call number (URI). You can check availability (GET), check out (POST), return (DELETE), or renew (PUT). The librarian (server) doesn't remember your previous requests—each transaction is independent (stateless).

## The Six REST Constraints

### 1. Client-Server Separation
The client (UI) and server (data/logic) are independent. You can change the mobile app without touching the backend, or scale the backend without updating clients.

**Why it matters:** Teams can work independently. Frontend devs don't need to know database schema.

### 2. Stateless
Every request from client to server must contain all information needed to understand and process it. The server doesn't store client session state between requests.

**Example:**
```java
// ❌ Stateful (NOT REST)
GET /api/login          // Server stores "logged in" state
GET /api/cart           // Relies on server remembering who you are

// ✅ Stateless (REST)
GET /api/cart
Headers:
  Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOi...
  // Every request includes auth token
```

**Why it matters:** Enables horizontal scaling (any server can handle any request), simplifies server logic, improves reliability (no session state to lose).

### 3. Cacheable
Responses must indicate whether they can be cached. Clients and intermediaries (CDNs, proxies) can cache responses to improve performance.

**Example:**
```java
@GetMapping("/api/products/{id}")
public ResponseEntity<Product> getProduct(@PathVariable Long id) {
    Product product = productService.findById(id);

    return ResponseEntity.ok()
        .cacheControl(CacheControl.maxAge(1, TimeUnit.HOURS))
        .eTag(String.valueOf(product.getVersion()))
        .body(product);
}
```

**Response Headers:**
```
Cache-Control: max-age=3600
ETag: "v123"
```

**Why it matters:** Reduces server load by 60-80% for read-heavy APIs, improves response time from 200ms to <10ms for cached responses.

### 4. Uniform Interface
The most important constraint. All resources follow the same interaction patterns:

**a) Resource Identification**: Each resource has a unique URI
```
GET /api/users/123              // User resource
GET /api/users/123/orders       // User's orders (sub-resource)
```

**b) Resource Manipulation through Representations**: Clients manipulate resources via representations (JSON, XML)
```java
// Client sends JSON representation
PUT /api/users/123
{
  "name": "John Doe",
  "email": "john@example.com"
}
```

**c) Self-Descriptive Messages**: Each message includes enough info to describe how to process it
```
Content-Type: application/json   // How to parse body
Accept: application/json         // What format client wants
Authorization: Bearer token      // Security context
```

**d) HATEOAS** (Hypermedia as the Engine of Application State): Responses include links to related actions
```json
{
  "id": 123,
  "name": "John Doe",
  "_links": {
    "self": "/api/users/123",
    "orders": "/api/users/123/orders",
    "delete": {
      "href": "/api/users/123",
      "method": "DELETE"
    }
  }
}
```

### 5. Layered System
Client doesn't know if it's connected directly to the server or through intermediaries (load balancers, caches, API gateways).

**Real Architecture:**
```
Client → CDN → API Gateway → Load Balancer → App Server → Database
```

**Why it matters:** Can add security layers, caching, load balancing without changing client code.

### 6. Code-On-Demand (Optional)
Server can send executable code (JavaScript) to extend client functionality.

**Example:** Server sends JavaScript for custom validation rules. Rarely used in modern APIs.

## What Makes an API "Truly RESTful"?

Most APIs claim to be REST but are really "HTTP APIs" that use some REST principles:

**Level 0 - RPC Style** (Not REST)
```
POST /api/getUser
POST /api/createUser
POST /api/updateUser
```

**Level 1 - Resources** (Basic REST)
```
POST /api/users/getUser
POST /api/users/createUser
```

**Level 2 - HTTP Verbs** (Most "RESTful" APIs)
```
GET    /api/users/123
POST   /api/users
PUT    /api/users/123
DELETE /api/users/123
```

**Level 3 - HATEOAS** (Truly RESTful)
```json
{
  "id": 123,
  "status": "pending",
  "_links": {
    "approve": "/api/orders/123/approve",
    "cancel": "/api/orders/123/cancel"
  }
}
```

Only Level 3 with HATEOAS is "truly RESTful" according to Fielding, but 99% of production APIs stop at Level 2 because:
- HATEOAS adds complexity
- Clients want predictable endpoints, not dynamic links
- Documentation with OpenAPI/Swagger is more practical

## REST in Spring Boot

```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/users")
public class UserController {

    @Autowired
    private UserService userService;

    // GET /api/users?page=0&size=20
    @GetMapping
    public ResponseEntity<Page<User>> getUsers(
            @RequestParam(defaultValue = "0") int page,
            @RequestParam(defaultValue = "20") int size) {

        Page<User> users = userService.findAll(PageRequest.of(page, size));
        return ResponseEntity.ok()
            .cacheControl(CacheControl.maxAge(5, TimeUnit.MINUTES))
            .body(users);
    }

    // GET /api/users/123
    @GetMapping("/{id}")
    public ResponseEntity<User> getUser(@PathVariable Long id) {
        return userService.findById(id)
            .map(user -> ResponseEntity.ok()
                .eTag(String.valueOf(user.getVersion()))
                .body(user))
            .orElse(ResponseEntity.notFound().build());
    }

    // POST /api/users
    @PostMapping
    public ResponseEntity<User> createUser(
            @Valid @RequestBody UserCreateRequest request) {

        User user = userService.create(request);

        URI location = ServletUriComponentsBuilder
            .fromCurrentRequest()
            .path("/{id}")
            .buildAndExpand(user.getId())
            .toUri();

        return ResponseEntity.created(location).body(user);
    }

    // PUT /api/users/123
    @PutMapping("/{id}")
    public ResponseEntity<User> updateUser(
            @PathVariable Long id,
            @Valid @RequestBody UserUpdateRequest request) {

        return userService.update(id, request)
            .map(ResponseEntity::ok)
            .orElse(ResponseEntity.notFound().build());
    }

    // DELETE /api/users/123
    @DeleteMapping("/{id}")
    public ResponseEntity<Void> deleteUser(@PathVariable Long id) {
        boolean deleted = userService.delete(id);
        return deleted ? 
            ResponseEntity.noContent().build() :
            ResponseEntity.notFound().build();
    }
}
```

## When to Use REST vs Alternatives

**Use REST when:**
- Building public APIs for external developers
- Need caching and HTTP infrastructure benefits
- CRUD operations on resources
- Mobile/web clients need simple, standardized API

**Consider alternatives:**
- **GraphQL**: When clients need flexible queries (avoid over-fetching)
- **gRPC**: For internal microservice communication (faster, type-safe)
- **WebSockets**: For real-time bidirectional communication
- **Server-Sent Events**: For server-push notifications

## Common REST Interview Follow-ups

**"Why is REST stateless?"**
Enables horizontal scaling (any server handles any request), simplifies failover (no session state to replicate), improves reliability.

**"How do you handle authentication in stateless REST?"**
JWT tokens in Authorization header. Each request includes the token. Server validates signature without database lookup.

**"Isn't HATEOAS impractical?"**
Yes, for most APIs. The industry has moved toward OpenAPI/Swagger documentation instead of runtime link discovery. Pure REST (Level 3) is rare in production.
